#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:10
msgid "Fixing Unicode across Python 2 and 3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:12
msgid ""
"This one took a while. Adding proper Unicode support to psutil took four "
"months of auditing, design decisions, and rewriting nearly every API that "
"returned a string. The full journey is documented in :gh:`1040`, and what "
"follows is a summary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:17
msgid ""
"This can serve as a case study for any Python library with a C extension "
"that needs to support both Python 2 *and* Python 3, as it will encounter the "
"exact same set of problems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:22
msgid "What was broken"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:24
msgid ""
"psutil has different APIs returning a string, many of which misbehaved when "
"it came to unicode. There were three distinctive problems (:gh:`1040`). Each "
"API could:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:28
msgid "**A**: raise a decoding error for non-ASCII strings (Python 3)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:29
msgid "**B**: return ``unicode`` instead of ``str`` (Python 2)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:30
msgid ""
"**C**: return incorrect / invalid encoded data for non-ASCII strings (both)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:32
msgid ""
":meth:`Process.memory_maps` hit all three on various OSes. :func:"
"`disk_partitions` raised decoding errors on every UNIX except Linux. Windows "
"service methods leaked ``unicode`` into Python 2 return values. The C "
"extension had accumulated years of ad-hoc encode/decode decisions, with no "
"single rule covering all of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:38
msgid "It was a mess."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:41
msgid "Filesystem or locale encoding?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:43
msgid ""
"First problem was that the C extension was using 2 approaches when it came "
"to decoding and returning a string: :c:func:`PyUnicode_DecodeFSDefault` "
"(filesystem encoding) for path-like APIs, and :c:func:"
"`PyUnicode_DecodeLocale` (user locale) for non-path strings like :meth:"
"`Process.username`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:48
msgid ""
"It appeared clear that I had to use :c:func:`PyUnicode_DecodeFSDefault` for "
"all filesystem-related APIs like :meth:`Process.exe` and :meth:`Process."
"open_files`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:52
msgid ""
"It was less clear, though, when to use :c:func:`PyUnicode_DecodeLocale`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:54
msgid ""
"After some back and forth, I decided to use a single encoding for all APIs: "
"the **filesystem encoding** (:c:func:`PyUnicode_DecodeFSDefault`). This "
"makes the encoding choice an implementation detail of psutil, not something "
"the user has to care about."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:60
msgid "Error handling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:62
msgid ""
"Second question was what to do in case the string cannot be correctly "
"decoded (because invalid, corrupted or whatever). On Python 3 + UNIX the "
"natural choice was ``'surrogateescape'``, which is also the default for :c:"
"func:`PyUnicode_DecodeFSDefault`. On Windows the default is "
"``'surrogatepass'`` (Python 3.6) or ``'replace'`` as per `PEP 529 <https://"
"www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0529/>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:69
msgid ""
"And here come the troubles: Python 2 is different. To correctly handle all "
"kinds of strings on Python 2 we should return ``unicode`` instead of "
"``str``, but I didn't want to do that, nor have APIs which return two "
"different types depending on the circumstance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:74
msgid ""
"Since unicode support is already broken in Python 2 and its stdlib (see :bpo:"
"`18695`), I was happy to always return ``str``, use ``'replace'`` as the "
"error handler, and simply consider unicode support in psutil + Python 2 "
"broken."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:79
msgid "Final behavior"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:81
msgid ""
"Starting from 5.3.0, psutil behaves consistently across all APIs that return "
"a string. The rules are intentionally simple, even if the underlying "
"implementation is not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:85
msgid ""
"The notes below apply to *any* method returning a string such as :meth:"
"`Process.exe` or :meth:`Process.cwd`, including non-filesystem-related "
"methods such as :meth:`Process.username`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:89
msgid ""
"all strings are encoded using the OS filesystem encoding (:c:func:"
"`PyUnicode_DecodeFSDefault`), which varies depending on the platform you're "
"on (e.g. ``'UTF-8'`` on Linux, ``'mbcs'`` on Windows)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:92
msgid "no API call is supposed to crash with :exc:`UnicodeDecodeError`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:93
msgid ""
"in case of badly encoded data returned by the OS, the following error "
"handlers are used to replace the bad characters in the string:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:96
msgid "Python 2: ``'replace'``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:97
msgid "Python 3: ``'surrogateescape'`` on POSIX, ``'replace'`` on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:99
msgid "on Python 2 all APIs return bytes (``str`` type), never ``unicode``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:100
msgid "on Python 2 you can go back to unicode by doing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:102
msgid ">>> unicode(proc.exe(), sys.getdefaultencoding(), errors=\"replace\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/unicode-internals.rst:106
msgid ""
"The full journey was implemented in :pr:`1052`, and shipped in 5.3.0 (see "
"the :ref:`changelog <530>`)."
msgstr ""
